<article class="env-remark" id="0AKL">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0AKL">Remark <span data-tag="0AKL">52.7.4</span>.</a> Here are some references to discussions of related material the literature. It seems that a “derived formal functions theorem” for proper maps goes back to <span class="cite">[<span class="postnote">Theorem 6.3.1</span>, <a href="/bibliography/lurie-thesis">lurie-thesis</a>]</span>. There is the discussion in <span class="cite">[<a href="/bibliography/dag12">dag12</a>]</span>, especially Chapter 4 which discusses the affine story, see More on Algebra, Section <a href="/tag/091N" data-tag="091N">15.93</a>. In <span class="cite">[<span class="postnote">Section 2.9</span>, <a href="/bibliography/G-R">G-R</a>]</span> one finds a discussion of proper base change and derived completion using (ind) coherent modules. An analogue of (<a href="/tag/0A0I" data-tag="0A0I">52.7.0.1</a>) for complexes of quasi-coherent modules can be found as <span class="cite">[<span class="postnote">Theorem 6.5</span>, <a href="/bibliography/HL-P">HL-P</a>]</span> </p>
